INT. ER TRAUMA BAY 2 - NIGHT

Fluorescent glare. Monitors scream. Blood on the floor tile.

A TRAUMA SURGEON, forties, gloved to the elbow, bends over a gurney. NURSE ABIOLA ADEYEMI, thirties, squeezes a bag-valve mask. RESIDENT DAVIT HOVSEPIAN, twenty-six, pumps the patient's chest. Anaesthetist DR. SUNITA RAO, fifties, calls out numbers. SECURITY GUARD LEROY, thirty, stands at the door. A bystander's mother, MRS. ELLINGTON, sobs behind him.

DR. RAO
Pressure's sixty over thirty!

DAVIT
Compressions continuing!

ABIOLA
Airway clear!

TRAUMA SURGEON
Clamp. I need a clamp.

DAVIT
Which clamp?

ABIOLA
The big one, Doctor Mendez!

MENDEZ
Thank you, Abiola.

Mendez takes the clamp. His hands move fast. Davit sweats through his scrubs, Abiola bags, Rao reads the monitor. Leroy blocks the door. Mrs. Ellington cries out and he lowers his head.

DR. RAO
Sixty-five over forty!

MENDEZ
Stay with me.

LEROY
Ma'am, you can't come in.

MRS. ELLINGTON
He's my boy!

DAVIT
Pulse!

ABIOLA
I've got a pulse!

The monitor settles into a steady beep. Mendez straightens. Everyone breathes.
